    "https://careers.und.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-of-forensic-science-tenure-track-grand-forks-north-dakota-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Forensic Science, Tenure Track",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Forensic Science, Tenure Track - Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Login/Settings EMPLOYEE RESOURCES Action Another action Something else here FINANCIAL SERVICES Action Another action Something else here OPERATIONS Action Another action Something else here COMMUNICATION SERVICES Action Another action Something else here EMPLOYEE RESOURCES Action Another action Something else here FINANCIAL SERVICES Action Another action Something else here OPERATIONS Action Another action Something else here COMMUNICATION SERVICES Action Another action Something else here Kiosk mode Assistant Professor of Forensic Science, Tenure Track Apply Now Assistant Professor of Forensic Science, Tenure Track 498876 Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Full-time Faculty Add to favorites View favorites Salary/Position Classification $76,000+, Dependent on Experience, 9-month contract, Exempt 40 hours per week This position will work onsite at UND Grand Forks, ND campus Purpose of Position The Forensic Science Program in the College of Arts & Sciences at the University of North Dakota invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor of Forensic Science. We seek candidates with an active, forensic science-focused research agenda, demonstrated by publications in peer-reviewed journals, presentations at professional conferences (e.g., AAFS), and the pursuit of external funding through submissions to state, federal, and other funding agencies. The successful candidate will be expected to have a 2/2 teaching load in the B.S. and M.S. Forensic Science programs, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, and submit at least one federal grant proposal within the first two years of employment. By Year 3, the teaching load will increase to a 2/3 depending on the level of external grant support. The successful hire will be expected to establish and sustain an externally funded research program focused primarily on forensic biology, contributing to the continued growth and national prominence of the Forensic Science Program. Established in 1883, the University of North Dakota is the oldest research university in North Dakota. Founded with a strong liberal arts foundation, it is the state’s flagship university and is classified by the Carnegie Foundation as having very high research activity. UND offers degrees in more than 225 fields of study and enrolls nearly 16,000 students. It is the home of the state’s only School of Law and School of Medicine. The School of Medicine and Health Sciences has major initiatives in rural and public health, and Aerospace is home to one of the first and largest unmanned aerial vehicle programs in the world. UND is located in Grand Forks, North Dakota, a vibrant college town of over 57,000 people located on the border of North Dakota and Minnesota. Short commutes, a great public-school system, high quality medical facilities, low crime, ample parks and theaters, and a cost of living below that found in large American cities are some of Grand Forks’ advantages. Ranked as one of the top 5 Best Small College Towns, and America’s Best Hockey Town, Grand Forks offers stellar quality of life without the metropolitan hassles. Duties & Responsibilities Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the B.S. and M.S. Forensic Science programs (typically a 2/2 or 2/3 teaching load) and mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Develop and maintain an active research program in forensic biology, including the publication of scholarly work and involvement of students in research activities. Pursue external funding to support research initiatives, including submission of at least one federal grant proposal within the first two years of employment. Maintain active engagement with the forensic science profession through scholarly presentations, professional service, and participation in organizations such as AAFS and ASCLD; promote the highest standards of scientific integrity, ethical practice, and professional conduct; and contribute to program, college, and university service. Required Competencies Excellent written and oral communication skills, including the ability to teach, inspire, and mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Demonstrated commitment to scientific integrity, ethical practice, professionalism, and responsible conduct in research, teaching, service, and student mentorship. Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills, with the ability to work effectively with students, faculty, and staff. Excellent organizational, time-management, and research management skills, including the ability to meet deadlines, and provide timely, professional, and constructive communication and feedback to students, colleagues, and program stakeholders. Proficiency with computer technologies and software applications relevant to teaching, research, and professional activities. Minimum Requirements Ph.D. in Forensic Science or a closely related field with a focus on Forensic Biology, completed by August 15, 2027. Successful completion of a Criminal History Background Check In compliance with federal law, all persons hired will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the US and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated expertise and experience in forensic biology, including research, applied forensic science practice, and/or experience in a forensic science laboratory. Evidence of teaching effectiveness at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Record of peer-reviewed publications demonstrating scholarly contributions to forensic biology / forensic science. Evidence of an active research agenda with the potential to establish and sustain an externally funded research program, demonstrated through successful acquisition of external funding and/or strong potential to secure funding in support of scholarly research activities. Evidence of scholarly dissemination through presentations at regional, national, or international scientific conferences (e.g., American Academy of Forensic Sciences Annual Scientific Conference). Active membership and engagement in professional organizations relevant to forensic science (e.g., AAFS, ASCLD, or related professional societies). Research interests that align with UND's Rural Health & Communities Grand Challenge (https://und.edu/research/grand-challenges/). To Apply To assure full consideration, applications must be received by 11/02/2026 and include the following materials: Cover letter. Curriculum Vitae, including academic credentials, professional experience, peer-reviewed publications, scholarly presentations, extramural funding, and professional activities and memberships (e.g., American Academy of Forensic Sciences). Research statement describing current research activities, future research plans, and potential opportunities for student involvement. Teaching philosophy statement. Contact information for three professional references. Unofficial transcripts. Please note that the selected candidate will be required to submit official transcripts upon hire. Position is open until filled. Questions can be directed to the Forensic Science Program Director, Lavinia Iancu at lavinia.iancu@und.edu. Please note, all employment postings close at 11:55pm CST. Position Benefits Benefits include single or family health care coverage (UND pays the full premium), life insurance, employee assistance program, retirement plans with generous employer contributions, sick leave in addition to paid holidays. Optional benefits include supplemental life, dental, vision, flexible spending account, supplemental retirement plans. UND also offers an employee tuition waiver and a variety of professional development opportunities . Find out more about UND's great benefits and perks ! Want to be notified of similar opportunities? First Name (required) 2942d4e4 Last Name (required) 472a246f Email (required) 1205954e Job Alert c9f74745 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Criminal Justice Criminal Justice Categories Tenure/Tenure-track Faculty Tenure/Tenure-track Faculty Employment Type Full-time Faculty Full-time Faculty Locations Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Send Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at UND.  Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies.  Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). By submitting your information, you acknowledge that you have read and agree to our Terms of Use . Thank you for your interest in applying to the University of North Dakota Categories: Tenure/Tenure-track Faculty Department: Criminal Justice Other UND Career Openings Assistant Professor, Strategic Communication, Tenure Track Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Communication Dept The Department of Communication at the University of North Dakota seeks a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Communication focusing on strategic communication strategic communication, such as advertising and public relations, who employs quantitative, qualitative, and/or computational research methods. Assistant Professor, Health & Science Communication, Tenure Track Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Communication Dept The Department of Communication at the University of North Dakota seeks a tenure-track assistant professor who has expertise in science and health communication within diverse social, cultural, political, and global contexts and who employs either qualitative, quantitative, and/or computational research methods. Department of Biomedical Sciences Chair and Professor, Tenured Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Biomedical Sciences The Chair of the Department of Biomedical Sciences will provide leadership and strategic direction for the department. This role involves overseeing and advancing the department's academic, research, and administrative functions, ensuring the highest standards of excellence in education, research, and service. Apply Now First Name (required) 62a32dea Last Name (required) c152a5d1 Email (required) 908e0a4b Apply Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at UND.  Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies.  Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). By submitting your information, you acknowledge that you have read and agree to our Terms of Use . Thank you Additional Information Find out why Grand Forks is Cooler . All information listed in this position announcement will be used by Human Resources, the Hiring Department, and Civil Rights & Title IX for screening, interviewing and selection purposes. Need help? Please email the Human Resources Department at UND.humanresources@UND.edu or contact us by phone at 701-777-4226. If you anticipate needing any type of accommodation to participate in any portion of the University's employment process, including completion of the online application process, please contact our office in advance of your participation or visit. Veteran’s Preference Veterans claiming preference must submit all proof of eligibility by the closing date. Proof of eligibility includes a DD-214 or a copy of NGB 22 from National Guard or Reserve (with a unit located in ND) or certification from the applicant's unit command that the individual is expected to be discharged or released from active duty in the uniformed services under other than dishonorable conditions not later than one hundred twenty days after the date of the submission of the certification. If claiming disabled veteran status, proof of eligibility includes a DD-214 and a current letter of disability dated within the past year. Confidentiality of Application Materials Pursuant to NDCC 44-04-18.27, applications and any records related to the applications that identify an applicant are confidential, except records related to the finalists of the position, which are open to the public after the search committee has identified the top three or more finalists who will be invited to campus. EEO Statement The University of North Dakota is an equal opportunity employer (veteran/disability). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, national origin (including shared ancestry/ethnicity), religion, sex, age, disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran's status, or other protected characteristic. Veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. Clery Statement In compliance with the  Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the University of North Dakota publishes an Annual Security and Fire Safety Report. The report includes the university’s policies, procedures, and programs concerning safety and security, as well as three years of crime statistics for our campus. As a prospective employee, you are entitled to a copy of this report. The report and statistical data can be found online at UND.edu. You may also request a paper copy of the report from the UND Police Department located at 3851 Campus Road, Grand Forks, ND, 58202. Before you go! Want to be notified of jobs as we post them? First Name (required) 70055a67 Last Name (required) eb5cd8f8 Email (required) 3d4d476d Job Alert e435636e Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Criminal Justice Criminal Justice Categories Tenure/Tenure-track Faculty Tenure/Tenure-track Faculty Employment Type Full-time Faculty Full-time Faculty Locations Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States Send Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at UND.  Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies.  Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). By submitting your information, you acknowledge that you have read and agree to our Terms of Use . Thank you for your interest in applying to the University of North Dakota Close Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalize content such as job recommendations, and to analyze our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device I do not accept I accept UND.humanresources@UND.edu | 701.777.4226 Careers at UND Staff Jobs Student Jobs Faculty Jobs Part-time & Temporary Positions © 2023 University of North Dakota - Grand Forks, ND - Member of ND University System Accessibility & Website Feedback Terms of Use & Privacy Notice of Nondiscrimination Student Disclosure Information Title IX Please choose the location(s)"
